These are licenses for different portions, not a choice of either license
for the entire repository. This file identifies scope; it adds no restrictions
to either standard license.

SCOPE RULES (apply to original material only)

1. Educational and documentary content — CC-BY-4.0
   Original prose, learning instructions, exercises, worksheets, case studies,
   reference annotations, diagrams and teaching slides in book/, diagrams/,
   labs/, references/, research/ and slides/ are educational content.
   Original Markdown documentation throughout the repository is also covered,
   including root README.md, docs/ (handbook guidance and project/review
   documentation), templates/, code/**/README.md, code/**/docs/*.md and
   website/*.md. Documentary plain-text records, including the controlled
   manifest in docs/, follow this rule. Templates are content, not programs.
   Original diagram source (including Mermaid), pseudocode, illustrative
   non-executable examples and original visual assets follow this rule.

2. Software and tooling — Apache-2.0
   Executable source and test code, scripts, build/dependency configuration,
   lockfiles, machine-readable test fixtures and automation data are software,
   wherever located. This includes code/ source, tests, fixtures and project
   configuration; tools/; tests/; website/ source, scripts, tests and build
   configuration; and repository configuration such as .gitignore.
   Machine-readable website/evidence/ validation records follow this rule.
   Source comments and API docstrings remain part of the software.

3. Mixed files and generated views
   In Markdown and teaching documents, original executable code examples
   (including shell commands, configuration and code fences containing actual
   software) are Apache-2.0; surrounding prose, pseudocode, illustrative
   output and Mermaid diagrams are CC-BY-4.0. A fenced block alone does not
   make prose or a diagram software.
   In website Astro/HTML/JavaScript files, original learner-facing prose is
   CC-BY-4.0; markup, styles, logic and embedded scripts are Apache-2.0.
   Thus website/src/pages/license.astro has educational/legal guidance copy
   under CC-BY-4.0 and its implementation under Apache-2.0. This also applies
   to learner-facing copy in other website components and pages.
   Rendered handbook/resource pages retain these source-level distinctions:
   displaying code in the handbook does not relicense it as CC-BY-4.0.
   Original website/public/favicon.svg and favicon.ico artwork is CC-BY-4.0;
   the software in website/public/ is Apache-2.0.
   Generated HTML and search assets preserve the respective rights in their
   underlying content, software and dependencies; generation is not relicensing.

THIRD-PARTY BOUNDARY

These grants apply only to original MSQE material for which Babatunde Ajala
holds copyright. They do not relicense third-party citations, standards,
quoted material, trademarks, external works, screenshots, code, assets,
dependencies or separately licensed material. Those retain their respective
rights and license terms, including any notices accompanying them. A citation
or link is not a grant of rights in the cited work. Standard license texts
are reproduced or referenced as their official terms, not MSQE-authored content.
No trademark rights are granted by this scope statement.

PRACTICAL EDUCATIONAL ATTRIBUTION

Modern Software Quality Engineering (MSQE) by Babatunde Ajala,
licensed under CC BY 4.0.

Include the source link (https://msqe.dev/ or the specific material's URL),
the license link (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), the supplied
copyright notice and any other required notices; indicate modifications.
This example is guidance, not an additional license condition. The standard
license controls the applicable attribution requirements.

For software redistribution, retain applicable notices and follow LICENSE-CODE.
